   "source": [
    "# Phase III — Scale anatomy, resolution robustness, artist geometry, and *The Starry Night*\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook starts **after the successful Phase I and Phase II experiments**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Its purpose is no longer to chase a higher classification score. It asks four scientific questions:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Which Gaussian scales carry the artist-discriminative curvature signal?**\n",
    "2. **Are the geometric descriptors stable when the same painting is resized?**\n",
    "3. **Which geometric descriptors differ most strongly among artists, with effect sizes and post-hoc contrasts?**\n",
    "4. **Where does *The Starry Night* lie within the empirical geometry of Van Gogh's corpus?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Two curvature implementations are deliberately kept separate:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Phase-I geometry** = the original 512 px smoothed-Sobel pipeline. It is reused for scale ablation, artist inference, and *Starry Night* positioning so those analyses remain directly comparable with the published experiment chain.\n",
    "- **Phase-III robustness geometry (`curvature_v2`)** = true derivative-of-Gaussian derivatives plus the dimensionless quantity\n",
    "  \\\[\n",
    "  \\tilde\\kappa_\\sigma=\\sigma_{\\rm px}\\kappa_\\sigma.\n",
    "  \\\]\n",
    "  For resolution comparisons, smoothing scales are matched *relatively*: a reference scale \\(\\sigma=2\\) at 512 px becomes 1 px at 256 and 4 px at 1024.\n",
    "\n",
    "This avoids silently changing the main benchmark while giving the resolution analysis a mathematically cleaner scale-space definition.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "bb46e3e6",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
